When I started playing Slash'EM after ascending a few characters in
NetHack, I wanted to fully experience the new things, so I chose a
doppelganger necromancer.  A long string of dead d.n.'s later, and I
switched to valkries, thinking it would be a little easier.  After
killing off a lot of those, I fired up one more doppelganger
necromancer, and it turned out to be my first Slash'EM ascension.

I started with a scoll of identify, a blessed scroll of charging, and
rings of levitation and regeneration.  I knew right away I'd better
play carefully to take advantage of such a great starting inventory.
Then I found a frozen food store on DL 2 (which is almost as good as a
ring of regeneration).  I stayed around DL1 and DL2 for a while to buff
up, then went down to DL3 . . . where I found a 0:2 wand of wishing
just laying on the ground.  Given my scrolls of ID and blessed
charging, I was able to nearly ascension-kit myself out immediately.

I proceeded VERY carefully after this, knowing this was almost surely
the best shot I'd ever have at ascending.  I did Sokoban first
(unneeded amulet of reflection), then went back to the mines.  I hadn't
found any altars in the main dungeon, and the minetown altar was
cross-aligned, so after donating once for AC I killed the priest and
set up camp.  I stayed there long enough to get Serpent's Tongue (a
permanently poisoned dagger, which I love) and some good spells, then
headed back to the main dungeon.  I still didn't have a bag of holding
by this point, so I wrested the final wish out of that wand for it.

Below Sokoban, I found a lot of altars.  I camped first on DL17 where I
did a little pudding farming (not much, since I didn't really need
anything), then later on DL29.  This would be my camp for the remainder
of the game.

I did the Chaotic and Neutral quests, gaining the Hand of Vecna
(wonderful, overpowered artifact) and the Eye of the Beholder (fun
artifact).  Everything went mostly smoothly from that point on.  I have
to say, even though I was a vanilla NetHack fanatic before, I think
Slash'EM is more fun (though it gets pretty far out there sometimes).
There is a lot more variety in levels (the main dungeon is much longer,
and many new special levels are scattered throughout), and a lot less
tedium in Gehennom (it's shorter, it's almost all special levels, and
there's no mysterious force).

Doppelganger necromancers are quite weak in the early game, but
ridiculously powerful in the mid-to-late game.  I used my
self-polymorph ability quite a lot, taking advantage of the abilities
of dragons, cockatrices, xorns, giants, vampires, and others.
Necromancers are intrinsically resistant to both level-drain and
sickness, which comes in VERY handy in the late game.

I nearly died when one of the major demons zapped a spell of create
pool under me in a hallway.  I luckily had one square to escape to, and
after that I wished up a pair of gauntlets of swimming and didn't take
them off.  That's really an evil spell.

I DID die a little later, by discovering Bug #1160101.  It's possible
to choke on a single corpse, starting from a not-satiated state.  Yuck.
 Thank you again, AoLS.  That's the only bug I ran into, though . . . I
saw none of the crashing problems I've read about.

Once I headed into Gehennom, the rest was nearly done in a single
session.  This was my first time seeing Demogorgon (you'll always see
him in Slash'EM), and even though I was resistant to sickness he was
STILL tough.  I can imagine he would be very difficult to beat without
that intrinsic.  The other demons were not much of a challenge, and I
blasted the newer, tougher Vlad from afar with two volleys from an
assault rifle and +6 bullets, so I can't comment on how tough he
actually was.  The vibrating square was a little more difficult to find
(the level is an undiggable maze), but everything else was just like
vanilla, from the sanctum through the planes (with the exception that
the sanctum was all the way down on level 61).

I tried to level-drain the riders so I could attempt taming, but they
weren't losing any levels after emptying whole wands into them and
casting Drain Life numerous times.  So, I opted to trap them and kill
them repeatedly until I was ready to leave.  In the map below, I'm at
the right altar, Death is locked in the left temple, and Famine and the
Pest are south of me, trapped between boulders.



